// §A6 last (§14.11.5 / §14.12.3) — `@no_auto_barrier`.
//
// The spec auto-emits a barrier after each sysreg / device-MMIO
// write so the caller doesn't have to remember which targets
// need an ISB / DSB / SFENCE.VMA on the trailing edge. That's
// the right default for ordinary code: it makes correctness
// the cheapest path.
//
// The one place the cost is worth eating is the kernel-init
// path's batch sysreg write, where the final write in the
// batch is itself a context-establishing instruction whose
// retire subsumes every prior barrier. On aarch64 that's the
// `SCTLR_EL1` write that turns the MMU on:
//
//   Sysreg.ttbr0_el1.write(newPgd)        // would auto-emit ISB
//   Sysreg.sctlr_el1.write(mmuEnable)     // its own ISB covers the prior
//
// Without the escape, every sysreg write in the batch carries
// its own ISB. With `@no_auto_barrier`, the user takes
// responsibility for the trailing barrier (or, as here, lets
// the final SCTLR write's implicit one do the job).
//
// The spec admits this only inside a function also attributed
// `@kernel_init` — that's the boot-path subset (post-@boot,
// pre-scheduler) where MMU bring-up runs. Sema rejects
// `@no_auto_barrier` anywhere else with a diagnostic pointing
// at the @kernel_init-only rule.
//
// v0.5 doesn't ship the Sysreg auto-barrier machinery yet (the
// typed Sysreg namespace from §14.12 queues for a later
// slice), so this attribute is a contract marker — the audit
// will read it and cross-check against the auto-emission
// table once that lands. The sema-side gate today records the
// boot-path-only constraint so misuse fails at compile time,
// not at audit time when it's harder to fix.

module examples.no_auto_barrier

// Canonical kernel-init MMU-bring-up shape. The @kernel_init
// attribute admits the function into the pre-scheduler regime;
// @no_auto_barrier opts out of the per-write barrier policy.
// v0.5 leaves the body as a stub since Sysreg.* aren't shipped
// yet; the type system / audit surface is what this slice
// pins.
@kernel_init
@no_auto_barrier
func enable_mmu_aarch64(_ ttbr0: UInt64, _ sctlr: UInt64) -> UInt64 {
    // In a real kernel: Sysreg.ttbr0_el1.write(ttbr0), then
    // Sysreg.sctlr_el1.write(sctlr). The hosted runtime just
    // returns the values so the e2e can observe the function
    // executed.
    return ttbr0 ^ sctlr
}

// Ordinary kernel-init code without the barrier opt-out keeps
// the policy. This is the spec's "default": correctness over
// micro-optimisation.
@kernel_init
func install_vector_table(_ vbar: UInt64) -> UInt64 {
    return vbar + 1
}
